    "evidence_text": "This site is easily and efficiently retargeted to other cellular and viral miRNAs by target site replacement.\n\nAsymmetric cryo-EM reconstruction of phage MS2 reveals genome structure in situ\n\nThis reveals that in situ, the viral RNA genome can adopt a defined conformation.\n\nThis suggests that genomic RNA is highly involved in genome packaging and virion assembly.\n\nThus, G. aurea is an excellent model organism for studying evolutionary mechanisms of genome contraction.   \n\n\nRESULTS Here we report sequencing and de novo draft assembly of G. aurea genome.\n\nProperties of Commelina yellow mottle virus's complete DNA sequence, genomic discontinuities and transcript suggest that it is a pararetrovirus.\n\nThe non-enveloped bacilliform viruses are the second group of plant viruses known to possess a genome consisting of circular double-stranded DNA.\n\nWe have characterized the viral transcript and determined the complete sequence of the genome of Commelina mellow mottle virus (CoYMV), a member of this group.\n\nAnalysis of the viral transcript indicates that the virus encodes a single terminally-redundant genome-length plus 120 nucleotide transcript.\n\nThe function of the 25 and 15 kd proteins is unknown.\n\nEach strand of the CoYMV genome is interrupted by site-specific discontinuities.\n\nBanana contains a diverse array of endogenous badnaviruses.\n\nbreeding programmes, as new hybrids are frequently infected with this virus, curtailing any further exploitation.\n\nCharacterization of complete genome and small RNA profile of pagoda yellow mosaic associated virus, a novel badnavirus in China.\n\nThe virus is tentatively named pagoda yellow mosaic associated virus (PYMAV).\n\nFurthermore, PYMAV vsRNAs were symmetrically distributed along the genome with no obvious vsRNA generating hotspots.\n\nSmallest angiosperm genomes found in lentibulariaceae, with chromosomes of bacterial size.\n\n4 Mbp).\n\nTaking into account these new measurements, genome size variation in angiosperms is now almost 2000-fold.\n\nC-values in Pinguicula varied only 1.7-fold from 487 to 829 Mbp.\n\nSri Lankan black pepper with symptoms of yellow mottle disease contained a mixture of viruses:\n\nAn effective purification procedure is described for PYMV.\n\nA sensitive polymerase chain reaction assay was developed to detect PYMV in black pepper.\n\nDetection of Sugarcane bacilliform virus isolate and its influence on yield and quality of cane in Yunnan\n\nPart sequence of replicase gene of the isolate SCBV-Kaiyuan was determined.",
